1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
One tender feed can mean different things
for different suppliers.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
TenderVerdict turns every notice into
a clear human next step.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Everything runs locally on this Mac
with synthetic demo data.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Before the review starts, the app shows
what was loaded and what is missing.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Each notice gets one of three clear results:
Open documents, Watch, or Reject.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The reasons stay visible, and a person
always makes the final decision.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The free version gives one complete supplier workflow:

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:52,000
the full queue, visible reasons, source links,
a review brief, and JSON export.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:59,000
RevenueCat unlocks Portfolio Workspace: comparison
and full export for up to five supplier profiles.

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:08,000
It never changes a verdict, creates a score,
or makes a bid decision.

11
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,000
In a verified RevenueCat Test Store run,
the SDK showed a test purchase screen.

12
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,500
CustomerInfo confirmed the entitlement was active.

13
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:23,000
It was not an App Store payment.
No money was charged.

14
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:31,000
For judges, RevenueCat Judge Access checks
the same entitlement without a purchase.

15
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:39,000
A fresh CustomerInfo check, Restore, returning to the app,
and a full relaunch all kept access active.

16
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,500
Synthetic demo data. No usable key is stored.
This is not production billing.

17
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:49,000
TenderVerdict is open-source macOS software
under Apache two point zero.
